.TH IOTSCAN 1 "2026-10-24" "iotscan 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
iotscan \- find vulnerable IoT devices on your home network
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B iotscan
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.RI [ TARGET ...]
.br
.B iotscan
.I COMMAND
.RI [ ARGS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B iotscan
discovers the devices on your local network, identifies what each one is running, and checks them for known vulnerabilities and risky configuration.
It ships with a checker for the October 2026 \fBPHP2Shell\fR flaws (CVE-2026-109618 and CVE-2026-109619), plus checks for end-of-life PHP, exposed phpinfo() pages, Telnet and UPnP.
New checkers are added as small Rust modules or, for banner-identifiable software, as entries in a TOML advisory file.
.PP
It is a single static binary with no runtime dependencies and works on Ubuntu, Lubuntu and other Linux systems (x86_64 and aarch64).
It runs as an ordinary user.
.PP
Run with no arguments it scans every local IPv4 subnet, prints a report, compares it with the previous scan, and saves the result.
.PP
.B Only scan networks you own or are authorised to test.
.SS Discovery
Devices are found by several independent methods and merged:
.IP \(bu 2
a TCP connect sweep (a refused connection still proves a host is alive);
.IP \(bu 2
the kernel ARP cache, which also reveals hosts that ignore every probe, and their MAC addresses;
.IP \(bu 2
an optional raw ARP sweep, when the binary has the CAP_NET_RAW capability (see \fBPRIVILEGES\fR);
.IP \(bu 2
mDNS / DNS-SD and SSDP / UPnP announcements, which name many smart-home devices;
.IP \(bu 2
IPv6 neighbour discovery by multicast echo to ff02::1.
.PP
MAC addresses are mapped to vendors with an embedded copy of the IEEE registry.
.SS Identification and checks
Open ports are identified (HTTP and HTTPS with self-signed certificates, SSH, Telnet, FTP, RTSP, MQTT).
Each web service is fetched once (following same-device redirects) and fingerprinted from headers, cookies and page content.
Checkers then judge the evidence.
.SS Confidence levels
Every finding has a severity (critical, high, medium, low, info) and a
.B confidence
that says how sure iotscan is that it applies to this device:
.TP
.B confirmed
Observed directly, for example an open Telnet port.
.TP
.B likely
Good evidence: an authoritative version in the affected range with no sign of vendor back-porting.
.TP
.B possible
A weak or indirect signal: the version is hidden, or the build looks like a Debian, Ubuntu or RHEL package, which usually back-ports fixes without changing the version number.
Check the package changelog or vendor notes before acting.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B scan
Scan the network. This is the default when no command is given.
.TP
.B gui
Start the local web interface and open it in a browser. See \fBGUI\fR.
.TP
.BI diff " OLD NEW"
Compare two saved scans (JSON files). Add \fB\-\-json\fR for machine-readable output.
.TP
.B history
List saved scans.
.TP
.B checkers
List the available checkers (\fB\-\-json\fR for JSON).
.TP
.B advisories
Show and validate the advisory database. \fB\-\-file FILE\fR merges and validates an extra file.
.TP
.B doctor
Report privileges, interfaces, and configuration problems. Run this first if something is missing.
.TP
.B selftest
Scan a fleet of mock devices on 127.0.0.1 and verify that the right findings (and no wrong ones) are reported. Takes about fifteen seconds and sends nothing off the machine.
.TP
.BI completions " SHELL"
Print a completion script for bash, zsh or fish.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Targets and ports
.TP
.I TARGET
What to scan. Any number of: an address (192.168.1.20), a CIDR block (192.168.1.0/24), a range (10.0.0.5-10.0.0.50 or 10.0.0.5-50), or an IPv6 address.
The default is every local IPv4 subnet (interfaces such as docker0 and veth* are skipped).
Subnets larger than \fB\-\-max\-hosts\fR addresses are narrowed to the block around your address, with a note.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-ports " " \fIPORTS\fR
Ports to sweep: \fBdefault\fR, \fBweb\fR, a list such as 80,443, or ranges such as 8000\-8100.
Ports needed by checkers (for example 23 for Telnet) are always added.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-iface " " \fIIFACE\fR
Use only this interface for auto-detection and multicast discovery.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-hosts " N"
Refuse to expand targets beyond N addresses (default 4096).
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-public
Confirm you are authorised to scan addresses outside private ranges (10/8, 172.16/12, 192.168/16, 169.254/16, loopback, fc00::/7, fe80::/10).
Without it, an interactive terminal asks for confirmation and non-interactive runs refuse.
.SS Behaviour
.TP
.B \-\-active
Send extra, still non-destructive requests: for example look for an exposed /phpinfo.php to learn a hidden PHP version.
iotscan never sends exploit payloads and never triggers memory-corruption bugs.
.TP
.B \-\-fast
More parallelism, no rate limit, shorter timeouts. Quicker, but harder on fragile devices.
.TP
.BI \-\-rate " N"
At most N new connections per second across the scan (default 400; 0 = unlimited).
.TP
.BI \-\-per\-host " N"
At most N simultaneous connections to one device (default 2).
.TP
.BI \-\-timeout\-ms " MS"
TCP connect timeout (default 1200).
.TP
.BR \-\-no\-mdns ", " \-\-no\-ssdp ", " \-\-no\-ipv6 ", " \-\-no\-arp
Disable mDNS, SSDP, IPv6 neighbour discovery, or the raw ARP sweep.
.TP
.BI \-\-only " IDS"
Run only these checkers (comma separated; see \fBiotscan checkers\fR).
.TP
.BI \-\-skip " IDS"
Skip these checkers.
.TP
.BI \-\-advisories " FILE"
Merge an extra advisory file over the built-in database.
.TP
.BI \-\-allowlist " FILE"
Accepted-risk suppressions (default: ~/.config/iotscan/allowlist.toml).
.SS Output
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the full result as JSON on standard output. The text report is not printed.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " " \fIFILE\fR
Also write the JSON result to FILE (mode 0600).
.TP
.BI \-\-html " FILE"
Write a standalone, script-free HTML report to FILE (mode 0600).
.TP
.BI \-\-color " WHEN"
\fBauto\fR (default), \fBalways\fR or \fBnever\fR. The NO_COLOR variable is honoured.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Show evidence and low-severity details.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Do not print progress.
.TP
.B \-\-show\-suppressed
Include findings suppressed by the allowlist.
.SS History
.TP
.B \-\-no\-history
Neither read nor write scan history.
.TP
.BI \-\-baseline " FILE"
Compare against this saved scan instead of the latest.
.TP
.BI \-\-history\-dir " DIR"
History directory (default: ~/.local/share/iotscan/history). The last 60 scans are kept.
.TP
.BI \-\-fail\-on " SEVERITY"
Exit with status 1 when an active finding at or above SEVERITY exists: \fBnone\fR, \fBinfo\fR, \fBlow\fR, \fBmedium\fR, \fBhigh\fR (default) or \fBcritical\fR.
.SH GUI
.B iotscan gui
starts a small web server bound to 127.0.0.1 only, prints a link, and opens it in your browser.
The interface shows your network as a panel of breakers, one per device, with tripped ones needing attention; a prioritised list of findings; a searchable device table; and per-device detail with advice.
It can start and stop scans, shows devices as they are found, compares with earlier scans, and exports the HTML report.
.PP
Options:
.BR \-\-port " " \fIN\fR
(default: a free port),
.B \-\-no\-open
(print the link, do not launch a browser),
.B \-\-demo
(show sample data without scanning),
.BR \-\-history\-dir ", " \-\-advisories ", " \-\-allowlist ", " \-\-no\-arp .
.PP
The link contains a random token that becomes an HttpOnly, SameSite=Strict cookie.
Every request must carry it, name 127.0.0.1 or localhost as its Host, and mutating requests must be same-origin JSON.
The page is served with a strict Content-Security-Policy and never inserts device-supplied text as markup.
Press Ctrl-C in the terminal, or use Quit in the page, to stop it.
.SH PRIVILEGES
.B iotscan
needs no privileges and should not be run with sudo.
Optionally, the faster raw ARP sweep can be enabled for your user:
.PP
.RS
sudo setcap cap_net_raw+ep $(command \-v iotscan)
.RE
.PP
The capability is used for one thing, opening ARP sockets, before any thread is started.
All capabilities are then dropped permanently, so everything that parses data from the network runs with none.
Replacing the binary clears the capability; run setcap again.
If started as root (for example via sudo) the same sockets are opened and the process then drops to the invoking user (SUDO_UID), or to nobody.
.SH SAFETY
.IP \(bu 2
Per-device (2) and global (256) concurrency limits and a global rate limit keep fragile devices alive.
.IP \(bu 2
Only GET, HEAD and OPTIONS requests are ever sent. Responses, headers and banners are size-capped, and every exchange has a deadline.
.IP \(bu 2
Redirects are followed only to the same device and port; SSDP description URLs are fetched only from the address that announced them.
.IP \(bu 2
TLS certificates are not verified (devices use self-signed certificates). No credentials or cookies are ever sent.
.IP \(bu 2
All text taken from the network is stripped of control characters before it is stored, so a hostile device cannot inject terminal escape sequences into reports. HTML output is escaped.
.IP \(bu 2
Checkers are pure functions over collected evidence and cannot perform I/O; a checker that panics is contained and reported.
.IP \(bu 2
Results (JSON, HTML, history) are written readable only by you.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success; no active finding at or above \fB\-\-fail\-on\fR.
.TP
.B 1
At least one active finding at or above \fB\-\-fail\-on\fR.
.TP
.B 2
Error (bad arguments, refused target, unreadable file).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/iotscan/history/scan-*.json
Saved scans (directory mode 0700, files 0600).
.TP
.I ~/.config/iotscan/allowlist.toml
Accepted risks. Example:
.PP
.RS
.nf
[[allow]]
device  = "aa:bb:cc:dd:ee:ff"   # MAC, IP address, or "*"
checker = "php2shell"           # optional
port    = 80                    # optional
reason  = "Vendor confirmed fix in firmware 2.4"
expires = "2027-03-01"          # optional, YYYY-MM-DD
.fi
.RE
.TP
.I ~/.config/iotscan/advisories.toml
Your own advisories, merged over the built-in database; entries with the same id replace built-in ones.
Example for software that names its version in a Server banner:
.PP
.RS
.nf
[[advisory]]
id = "examplehttpd-2026"
product = "examplehttpd"        # from "Server: ExampleHTTPd/1.2.3"
title = "ExampleHTTPd request smuggling"
severity = "high"
cves = ["CVE-2026-00000"]
summary = "..."
remediation = "Upgrade to 1.4.2 or later."
[[advisory.affected]]
branch = "1.4"
fixed = "1.4.2"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Validate with \fBiotscan advisories\fR.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Disable colour output.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME, XDG_DATA_HOME
Override the config and data directories.
.TP
.B COLUMNS
Terminal width when it cannot be detected.
.SH EXAMPLES
Scan everything, then see what changed:
.PP
.RS
iotscan
.RE
.PP
Look harder at one camera, including exposed phpinfo() pages:
.PP
.RS
iotscan 192.168.1.20 \-\-active \-v
.RE
.PP
Weekly cron job that exits non-zero on critical findings and writes a report:
.PP
.RS
iotscan \-q \-\-fail\-on critical \-\-html ~/iot-report.html
.RE
.PP
Only the PHP checks, as JSON, for a script:
.PP
.RS
iotscan \-\-only php2shell,php\-eol \-\-json | jq '.devices[].findings[]'
.RE
.PP
Try the interface without scanning anything:
.PP
.RS
iotscan gui \-\-demo
.RE
.SH LIMITATIONS
Banners are claims.
Distributions back-port fixes without changing version numbers, and some devices hide or fake their headers, so version-based findings carry a confidence level and should be verified.
A device that hides its PHP version entirely can be reported only as \fBpossible\fR.
IPv6 neighbour discovery uses unprivileged ICMP sockets and needs net.ipv4.ping_group_range to include your group.
iotscan can see only what is reachable from where it runs: devices on another VLAN or a guest network need a scan from there.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR setcap (8),
.BR nmap (1),
.I docs/WRITING_CHECKERS.md
in the source distribution.
